            <ArticleTitle>Tolerance to etoricoxib in 37 patients with urticaria and angioedema induced by nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>168-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">The use of cyclooxygenase-2 inhibitors, a new class of analgesic drugs, is suggested in patients with hypersensitivity to nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate tolerance to etoricoxib, a new cyclooxygenase-2 inhibitor, in NSAID-sensitive patients with urticaria-type adverse reactions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Thirty-seven patients with adverse reactions to NSAIDs.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Single-blind, placebo-controlled oral challenge with increasing doses of etoricoxib.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Thirty-four patients tolerated etoricoxib treatment without adverse reactions, but a generalized urticarial rash developed in 3 patients (8%).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Etoricoxib, like other cyclooxygenase-2 inhibitors, is a well-tolerated drug in most NSAID-sensitive patients. However, according to our experience, a previous challenge test in a safe environment may be necessary before prescribing the drug to such patients.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
